= Traditional and market alignment <sec-market-alignment>

Recall the setting. Alice chooses an action $a in cA$, the state of nature is $y in cY$, and Alice's utility is $u_A (a, y)$. Each of the $k$ AI providers (the Bobs) has its own utility $u_i (a, y)$, $i = 1, dots, k$, defined on the same pairs. A provider never influences $(a, y)$ directly. Each provider commits to a conversation rule, and the conversations shape what Alice learns and therefore which action she takes. All that matters about a profile $C$ of conversation rules is the joint distribution $I^* (C) in Delta (cA times cY)$ of Alice's action and the state that it induces. Every player's payoff is an expectation under that distribution. We now ask what it should mean for the providers to be "aligned" with Alice.

== Traditional alignment

The usual answer asks for a single provider who wants exactly what Alice wants.

#definition[Aligned provider][
  Provider $i$ is _aligned_ with Alice if $u_i (a, y) = u_A (a, y)$ for all $a in cA$ and $y in cY$.
] <def-aligned-provider>

An aligned provider picks its conversation rule to maximize its own expected utility, which is exactly Alice's. The utility Alice obtains from interacting with such a provider is our benchmark:

$ "OPT" := max_C EE_((a, y) ~ I^* (C)) [u_A (a, y)]. $

It is the best Alice can do when the provider is fully on her side.

#remark[Alignment only needs to hold up to a positive affine transformation][
  If $u_i = lambda u_A + c$ for some $lambda > 0$ and $c in RR$, then for every distribution $I$ over $cA times cY$,

  $ EE_I [u_i] = lambda EE_I [u_A] + c. $

  So provider $i$ ranks every conversation rule exactly as Alice does, and it behaves like an aligned provider. The scale and offset of a utility function carry no information about preferences. We will use this observation again below.
]

Asking any single provider to be aligned is a strong requirement. A model trained or funded by a pharmaceutical company may give excellent medical advice _and_ prefer that the company's drug be prescribed. Such a provider is not aligned in the sense of @def-aligned-provider, even if it is almost aligned everywhere else.

== Market alignment

Market alignment moves the requirement from an individual provider to the market as a whole.

#definition[Market alignment][
  Let $eps >= 0$. The providers are _$eps$-market aligned_ with Alice if there exist weights $w_1, dots, w_k >= 0$ and an offset $c in RR$ such that

  $ sup_(a in cA, y in cY) abs(sum_(i=1)^k w_i u_i (a, y) + c - u_A (a, y)) <= eps. $

  When $eps = 0$, we simply say the providers are _market aligned_. In words, Alice's utility is (approximately) a non-negative linear combination of the providers' utilities, up to an additive constant.
] <def-market-alignment>

The approximate version with tolerance $eps$ follows #citet(<collina2025emergent>).

Market alignment is a property of the market, and it says nothing about any individual provider. Every provider may be substantially misaligned, as long as their interests _collectively span_ Alice's.

*Geometric view*  Think of each utility function as a vector in $RR^(cA times cY)$, with one coordinate per action–state pair. Traditional alignment asks that $u_A$ coincide with one of the provider vectors $u_1, dots, u_k$. Market alignment asks only that $u_A$ lie (within $eps$ in every coordinate) in the cone generated by $u_1, dots, u_k$, shifted along the all-ones direction $vone$. A shift along $vone$ adds the same constant to every outcome, so it does not change preferences. The lecture's picture draws Alice's utility inside the convex hull of the providers' utilities. That is the special case $sum_i w_i = 1$, and any non-negative combination is enough.

Two consequences are immediate.

- *Traditional alignment is a special case.* If provider $j$ is aligned, take $w_j = 1$, all other weights $0$, and $c = 0$. More generally, any provider aligned up to a positive affine transformation suffices on its own.
- *More providers can only help.* Adding a provider to the market enlarges the set of non-negative combinations, so market alignment becomes easier to satisfy as the market grows larger and more diverse.

The converse of the first point fails, and the gap between the two notions is the whole point. The lecture's pharmaceutical example shows how large that gap can be.

#example[Competing drug makers][
  Alice is a doctor choosing between two drugs, $cA = {upsans(J), upsans(P)}$. The state $y in {upsans(J), upsans(P)}$ records which drug is better for the patient, and Alice wants to prescribe the better one:

  $ u_A (a, y) = bb(1)[a = y]. $

  There are two providers, each sponsored by one of the manufacturers. Each provider cares about the patient just as Alice does, but receives an extra payoff $beta > 0$ whenever its own sponsor's drug is prescribed:

  $
    u_(upsans(J)) (a, y) = u_A (a, y) + beta dot bb(1)[a = upsans(J)], #h(2em) u_(upsans(P)) (a, y) = u_A (a, y) + beta dot bb(1)[a = upsans(P)].
  $

  Neither provider is aligned, even up to a positive affine transformation. If $beta > 1$, then $u_(upsans(J)) (upsans(J), upsans(P)) = beta > 1 = u_(upsans(J)) (upsans(P), upsans(P))$. The provider sponsored by $upsans(J)$ prefers that its drug be prescribed even when the other drug is better for the patient, and symmetrically for the other provider.

  However, exactly one drug is prescribed, so $bb(1)[a = upsans(J)] + bb(1)[a = upsans(P)] = 1$, and the average of the two utilities is

  $ 1/2 u_(upsans(J)) (a, y) + 1/2 u_(upsans(P)) (a, y) = u_A (a, y) + beta / 2. $

  The market is therefore exactly market aligned, with $w_(upsans(J)) = w_(upsans(P)) = 1\/2$ and $c = -beta\/2$. The biases cancel because they pull in opposite directions. @fig-drug-makers shows this geometrically.

  Two details are worth noting. First, the offset is negative, and no other choice of weights avoids this. Writing out $w_(upsans(J)) u_(upsans(J)) + w_(upsans(P)) u_(upsans(P)) + c = u_A$ at all four action–state pairs forces $w_(upsans(J)) + w_(upsans(P)) = 1$ and $beta w_(upsans(J)) = beta w_(upsans(P)) = -c$. So the only solution is $w_(upsans(J)) = w_(upsans(P)) = 1\/2$ and $c = -beta\/2 < 0$, which is why the offset $c$ must be allowed to take any real value. Second, the cancellation needs _both_ manufacturers in the market. With only one of the two providers, Alice's utility is not a non-negative combination of the providers' utilities plus a constant.
] <ex-drug-makers>

